A||||10||peterh||question for theogre on steering column - long post||||1||
Z||000000||peterh||08-22-2000||09:05 AM||||Back in May I posted a problem with starting my car and you sent me a message about it being in the steering column. I also looked at your web site, very informative. As it sounded rather tricky to work on the steering column and the car started 99% of the time I did nothing about it at the time. <BR>I still have a very intermittent problem with my 86 SE V6 auto with tilt steering where the key will turn but I do not get a click or anything. I feel the normal resistance in the switch and the gauges show that there is electrical power. This has only happened 3 times since I have had the car (1 year). The first time the car started normally after waiting 5 minutes. The next time it would not start after 1 hour and I had the car towed home. Of course it started fine the next morning. I then replaced the starter motor figuring it was a heat soaked starter problem. I did not drive it for a while after that due to 2 months of rain here in N.Y.  <BR>Recently I drove it approx. 45 minutes , stopped at the grocery store , when I tried starting it nothing. I waited 10 minutes and fortunately it started. I then took it to my local mechanic. He checked the battery, wires , put it on his diagnostic equipment and found nothing wrong. I mentioned the ignition switch to rack gear possibility to him and he did not think that was the problem.<BR>I am now resigned to working on the steering column myself, making or buying the  tools needed. <BR>The big question is why would this problem seem to be heat related or is this just a red herring.<BR>Any advice?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||08-22-2000||11:11 AM||||I would think that contrary to what your mechanic says the park/neutral switch should be looked at. Have you tried to start it in neutral?||||reg||1
Z||000002||peterh||08-22-2000||12:33 PM||||Yes, I did try it in neutral. One thing I did not try was moving the tilt steering up and down the last time it happened. Also the car starts fine after sitting in the sun all day with the interior temp over 100, so the the heat issue may be misleading.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-22-2000||12:51 PM||||Normal resitance to turning key....... It could be a number of things. P/N switch, ignition switch, starter, batery cables..... It's posible it's a mechanicle problem in the column but I'd look at the ignition switch AFTER doing the obvious and cleaning the battery cable terminals. (trace the cables and clean all the terminals. battery ends block ends and connections to the body.)<P>It could be a simple issue with the ignition switch. It's on top of the column. You have to drop the column to get to it but you don't need any thing special for that. It's just 2 nuts and 2 bolts that hold the braket to the dash frame. (Don't remove the 4 bolts that hold the bracket to the column. won't drop like that.)<P>The switch could be loose misadjusted or it may just need replacement. I think the switch is slightly adjustable. I don't remember right now.<P>The grease in them gets crappy over time and can interfere with getting a good contact. Heat can affect it, especially if it's the grease crapped up. They do just flat wear out as well. So in this case start with the switch.<P>If you take the switch off follow the warning in the cave article about the shifter interlock cable..... ||||reg||1
Z||000004||peterh||08-22-2000||03:07 PM||||Thanks for the info and support. I just ordered an ignition switch from the Fiero Store. The Fiero Store sounded real busy which is encouraging for us Fiero owners. I will hopefully install the switch this weekend. I am still finishing my kitchen and cannot dedicate the time to my Fiero thatI want to.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex4mula||08-22-2000||04:02 PM||||I had to replace the ignition switch in my Formula about 3 years ago. But in my case every day I had to turn the switch harder to make it start. Waited too long and had to take the lock/key guy to the parking lot. $150  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Patrick||08-22-2000||08:26 PM||||<BR>It's been almost three years since I had mine apart (so don't ask for details <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">), but I had a similar problem. It wasn't the starter switch at fault, but it was simply an adjustment needed for the rod that goes between the <I>actual</I> starter switch and the ignition switch. I emphasized the word <I>actual</I> in the last sentence because the ignition switch is NOT the starter switch. Is this making any sense? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-22-2000||10:50 PM||||Patrick, I think you mean the key lock isn't the ignition/starter switch.<P>The only "switch" that is part of the keylock is the contact that rings the chime when you open the door with the key in the lock. All other switch function is in the switch on the other end of the rod that you had to adjust.<P>One of 4 things usually happens to screw up the linkage between the keylock and the ignition switch.<P>1. the key lock gets worn. The extra play in the lock can be enough to keep the starter contact from biting right.<BR>2. the gears wear. same result as 1.<BR>3. the ignition switch goes bad or it's mounting nuts get loose. <BR>4. the tilt section of the link cracks or breaks out right. (Only in tilt column.) There is a "loop and hook" in the linkage on tilt sets. Usually the loop breaks or wears out in one spot.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Patrick||08-23-2000||02:16 AM||||<BR>Thanks Ogre, I'm glad you knew what the heck I was talking about. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I just wanted to make sure some way or another that people didn't start replacing the <I>key-lock</I> if the problem was actually the <I>ignition/starter switch</I> or vice versa. You've clarified things well as usual.||||reg||14
Z||000009||Carrolles||08-23-2000||08:31 AM||||Try Oliver Scholz's web page for a good write up on getting into the steering column.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/fieroidx.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/fieroidx.html</A> <P>BTW Oliver Scholz's web site of articles should be on everyone's Favorite's list.<P>Thanks Oliver !!!<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000010||prof bobo||08-23-2000||02:02 PM||||When my ignition switch died, and I discovered  how much the replacement part would cost, I replaced it with a couple of toggle switches for the IGN and ACCY modes and a pushbutton for START.  A relay cuts out radio and fan circuits in START mode. Put a keyswitch in series with the start button, left the column unlocked (hid a toggle swith under the dash, so I don't really 'need' a key to start it).  Relocated the mess to the center console between the window switches, with lighted switches, the effect is kinda cool -hop in, flip a switch, press a button and go.  Plus I  no longer need the key to open/close the windows.  Hot-wiring it now would be an . . . adventure.||||reg||1
